<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">What Is Cervical Spondylosis?</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Cervical spondylosis is a degenerative condition of the cervical spine, the seven vertebrae (C1 through C7) that form the neck. Over time the discs between these vertebrae lose their water content and height, the cartilage on the small joints of the spine wears down and the bone may develop small spurs in response. Together these changes reduce the flexibility and shock-absorbing capacity of the neck.</p>
  <p>The cervical spine carries the weight of the head, allows the neck to bend, straighten, tilt and rotate and protects the spinal cord running through it. Degenerative changes in this area can press on the nerves leaving the spine, narrow the spinal canal and restrict normal neck movement.</p>
  <p>Cervical spondylosis is one of the most common age-related spinal conditions. Changes may begin from the thirties onwards, though symptoms usually become noticeable after 40. It is not a single disease but a range of degenerative changes and the presentation differs depending on which segments are affected, how advanced the wear is and whether nerves are involved.</p>
</section>

<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Types of Cervical Spondylosis</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <div class="cs-types-grid">
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Cervical Disc Degeneration</h4>
      <p>The discs between the neck vertebrae lose water content, become thinner and develop small tears in the tough outer ring. The disc then spreads load less evenly, which puts extra stress on the joints and bone around it. This most commonly affects the lower neck segments, which take the greatest load during movement. A bulging or herniated disc may follow when degenerated disc material pushes beyond its normal boundary.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Cervical Osteoarthritis</h4>
      <p>Wear of the small joints on either side of the spinal column. As the protective cartilage thins, bone contact develops and inflammation and bone spurs follow. This usually causes localised neck pain, stiffness that worsens after holding one posture and a grinding sensation on movement. It often occurs alongside disc degeneration.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Cervical Stenosis</h4>
      <p>Narrowing of the spinal canal or of the openings through which nerves exit the spine. It can follow bone spur formation, disc bulging or thickening of the spinal ligaments. When the canal narrows the spinal cord may be compressed. When the nerve openings narrow, individual nerve roots are compressed, producing pain, numbness or tingling that travels into the shoulder, arm and hand.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Cervical Myelopathy</h4>
      <p>The most serious form, where degenerative changes compress the spinal cord itself. It may cause difficulty with fine tasks such as buttoning clothes or writing, problems with balance and walking, weakness in the legs and, in advanced cases, bladder or bowel changes. This presentation needs careful clinical assessment and may require multidisciplinary management so early identification matters.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

