<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Common Causes That Contribute to Cervical Spondylosis</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Cervical spondylosis develops from a combination of age-related wear and lifestyle factors. Contributing causes include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Age-related disc dehydration and vertebral wear that begins in the thirties and progresses over time</li>
    <li>Prolonged forward head posture and poor ergonomics during desk work, computer use and phone use, which load the lower neck segments</li>
    <li>Occupations involving repetitive neck movement, overhead work, long screen hours, manual labour or driving</li>
    <li>Previous neck injury such as whiplash, a fall or sports trauma, which can accelerate degeneration at the affected segment</li>
    <li>Genetic predisposition, with some people showing earlier and more pronounced degeneration than others of the same age</li>
    <li>Smoking, which reduces blood supply to the discs and impairs the nutrition they need</li>
    <li>A sedentary routine with weak neck muscles, which reduces the support the muscles give the spine</li>
  </ul>
</section>